from pathlib import Path
import json, hashlib, re
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
P=json.loads((ROOT/'input-contract.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig'))
def write(name,text): (ROOT/name).write_text(text.strip()+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
def dump(name,obj): write(name,json.dumps(obj,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False))
def sha(name): return hashlib.sha256((ROOT/name).read_bytes()).hexdigest()

def copyedit_anchor_prose(text):
 # Apply only to inherited prose, never IDs, formulas, paths, or the input contract.
 text=re.sub(r'\b(sequence|times|nominal|setting|exposure|row|is|count|ratio|time|display|by|valid|invalid|center|difference|sum|mean|duration|at|the)(?=\d)',r'\1 ',text)
 text=text.replace('A10-significant-digit','A 10-significant-digit')
 text=re.sub(r'(\d)(nm|s)\b',r'\1 \2',text)
 text=re.sub(r'\b(counts|Clear|are|and|pair|offsets|is|detail|window)(?=\()',r'\1 ',text)
 text=re.sub(r'\b(support|in|to)(?=\[)',r'\1 ',text)
 text=text.replace(']s','] s').replace(',Clear',', Clear').replace(',R a',', R a').replace('s,1 s','s, 1 s')
 text=text.replace('independent box bounds','the stated box of allowed combinations')
 return text

explanations=[
'''A photometer turns light arriving at a detector into a recorded number. A channel is one named response pathway of that instrument. A wavelength label describes where a response is nominally located; it does not say that the detector accepts only that exact wavelength. A nanometre (nm) is one billionth of a metre. Keep the response identity even when a full response curve is unavailable.

Counts are the instrument's numeric output. Gain is an instrument setting that changes how the input becomes output. Exposure is the duration over which a reading is collected. Calibration is a documented relation between an instrument output and a specified reference quantity under stated conditions. Without that relation, counts remain counts. Dividing by exposure can produce counts per second, but does not manufacture watts, a unit of power.

Orientation specifies which way the sensor faces. Here +z means the positive third axis of a declared toy laboratory frame: x points right on the bench, y points away from the observer, and z points upward. The sensor normal is the perpendicular arrow out of its receiving surface. This toy frame is not an astronomical frame.

A midpoint timestamp locates the middle of an exposure, not the entire reading at an instant. For duration e, half lies before the midpoint and half after it: support is [t-e/2,t+e/2]. Square brackets include the endpoints. In this course t is elapsed seconds from a synthetic session start. It is not a UTC epoch. One real run would need both its UTC start and its clock convention.

Our common acquisition is synthetic device T, session A, gain setting 1, exposure e=1/5 s, fixed +z normal, A nominal 500 nm, and broadband Clear response identity C-toy. No calibration is supplied. Rows 0 through 4 have midpoint times 0,1,2,3,4 s, A counts 2,5,8,1,3 and Clear counts 10,0,20,10,10. These values were authored for arithmetic. Read the list in order: position 0 is the first row, not row 1.''',
'''A raw ledger is the record of what was supplied before an analysis changes its representation. A row key tells us which acquisition a value belongs to. Use device, session, sequence number and channel together; a timestamp alone may repeat. A sequence number records acquisition order even if a later display is sorted by time.

Separate three situations. A recorded zero is a value. A null is an explicit entry saying no usable numeric value was supplied. An absent expected sample has no acquired row; its gap belongs in a parallel expectation ledger. None may be replaced by the others. A gap is meaningful only relative to a declared expected schedule.

A fault flag states a diagnostic condition and its origin. It does not change the stored value. For practice, independently attach suspected saturation to row 3 with reason “synthetic instrument-status exercise.” Saturation means an instrument may have reached a response limit; the small count 1 does not itself establish saturation. We are practicing a flagged branch, not diagnosing this count from its size. Retain the reason and the fact that the flag was stipulated.

A mask is a parallel list of inclusion or validity labels for a particular view. Its purpose must be named: a ratio-validity mask is not a raw-acquisition validity mask. A corrected view, if independently justified, gets a new field and transformation record. Keeping the original key lets us join every result back to its input.

To make sorting reversible, record the original sequence beside each displayed row. For example, if rows appear in order 2,0,1, their original sequence labels restore 0,1,2. Duplicate timestamps remain separate keys. No large source copy is needed: for real archives preserve an immutable source path and hash, then store only authored derived records and their keys.''',
'''A ratio answers “how much A per unit of Clear in this paired reading?” It does not answer how much absolute light arrived. Let C mean the Clear count in the same acquisition row, and define r=A/C when both inputs are present and finite and C is nonzero. This is the mathematical domain. A stricter instrument implementation may require positive C; record that policy separately. The installed Clear-Normalize tool uses the stricter nonpositive-C invalid branch. Our toy valid denominators are all positive, so the policies agree here.

The units count/count cancel, so r is dimensionless. Do not divide times, sequence numbers or orientations by C. Where C is valid, Clear/C=1. Do not clip a ratio greater than 1: A=12,C=10 gives 1.2 and remains a ratio. A negative spectral input stays signed with its instrument status. Undefined is a status, not a large number or zero.

An inverse account explains how to recover inputs. If r is exact, A=rC. The pair (r,C) preserves amplitude; r alone does not, because (2,10) and (4,20) both give 1/5. For an invalid ratio retain the raw A and C directly. They are still available for other analyses.

Displayed decimals may be rounded. Ten significant digits count from the first nonzero digit. Keep exact fractions when possible. If r_display is rounded, define the signed ratio closure delta=r_exact-r_display. Then A=(r_display+delta)C. For 1/3, 0.3333333333 equals 3333333333/10000000000. Subtracting from 1/3 gives (10000000000-9999999999)/30000000000=1/30000000000. Multiplying the display alone by 3 gives 0.9999999999; adding the retained closure recovers 1 exactly.

For the common acquisition, row 0 gives 2/10=1/5 and returns (1/5)10=2; row 2 gives 8/20=2/5 and returns 8; row 3 gives 1/10 and returns 1; row 4 gives 3/10 and returns 3. Row 1 has A=5,C=0, so no division is performed. Keep its pair (5,0), the undefined-ratio flag and all original metadata.''',
'''A reflection compares positions equally far from a specified center. For a four-entry list x=(x0,x1,x2,x3), choose the center halfway between indices 1 and 2. Reversal R sends x to (x3,x2,x1,x0). It is an operation on positions, not a change of sign. Applying R twice returns the original list.

Define s=(x+Rx)/2 and a=(x-Rx)/2, adding and subtracting corresponding entries. The same component satisfies Rs=s; the inverted component satisfies Ra=-a. Adding the definitions cancels Rx and returns x=s+a. Same-polarity defect x-Rx equals 2a; inverted defect x+Rx equals 2s. Both defects are lists, not merely scores. An exactly same input has zero same defect; an exactly inverted input has zero inverted defect. Neither condition requires the other.

For x=(1,2,4,3), Rx=(3,4,2,1). The sum is (4,6,6,4), so s=(2,3,3,2); the difference is (-2,-2,2,2), so a=(-1,-1,1,1). Addition gives (1,2,4,3), entry by entry. Keep the nonzero defects (-2,-2,2,2) and (4,6,6,4). The words same and inverted refer only to this operator and center.

A periodic half-turn is different. For four equally spaced positions on a circle, H shifts two places: Hx=(x2,x3,x0,x1). E means the even-part operation Ex=(x+Hx)/2; O means the odd-part operation Ox=(x-Hx)/2. I is the identity operation Ix=x, so E+O=I means their outputs add back to the input. HEx=Ex and HOx=-Ox. Here parity means behavior under H, not chirality or any other source parity.

An even comparator has form c=(p,q,p,q). Every circular shift has that form with some pair of values. Any odd vector has form o=(u,v,-u,-v). Their dot product means multiply matching entries and add: c dot o=pu+qv-pu-qv=0. Thus shifting and comparing this comparator can never recover odd amplitudes. This is comparator blindness, also called a nullspace: inputs mapped to zero by the comparison. Even large odd structure is invisible to it. A weak channel deserves the same decomposition and retention as a strong one.

We also teach a small frequency encoding so that keeping phase has a concrete meaning. A complex number a+bi stores two signed coordinates, with i*i=-1. Addition is coordinatewise; multiplication distributes, replacing i*i by -1. No calculus is needed. For four samples, define the discrete Fourier transform (DFT) by four weighted sums:

F0=x0+x1+x2+x3; F1=x0-i*x1-x2+i*x3; F2=x0-x1+x2-x3; F3=x0+i*x1-x2-i*x3.

The inverse is x0=(F0+F1+F2+F3)/4; x1=(F0+i*F1-F2-i*F3)/4; x2=(F0-F1+F2-F3)/4; x3=(F0-i*F1-F2+i*F3)/4. These declared signs and the factor 1/4 are part of the contract. Frequency index j counts cycles across the four-sample period. Indices differing by 4 give identical weights (aliasing); this grid cannot distinguish native modes 25 and 27 from indices 1 and 3.

For x=(1,2,3,4), Ex=(2,3,2,3), Ox=(-1,-1,1,1). The odd transform is (0,-2+2i,0,-2-2i): for example F1=-1+i-1+i=-2+2i. Inverting gives x0=(-4)/4=-1, x1=(-4)/4=-1, x2=4/4=1, x3=4/4=1 for the odd component. Every shift of Ex has zero dot product with Ox. Retain both complex coefficients and the even component; a zero comparator result cannot replace them.

Source card S05 R017 describes odd-harmonic structural zeros of an antipodally symmetric synthetic comparator. R021 separately synthesizes a native ternary ladder 1,3,9,27 (each is three times its predecessor) and its comparator blindness. R028 retains 25–27→2 as a proposed difference pathway: 27-25=2 is arithmetic; physical coupling is an additional claim. The Fibonacci-cutoff proposal is a separate source branch, not a consequence of our four-point DFT. These archive claims are not fresh native-data replay. See sources.md for exact pointers and limitations.''',
'''An event rule identifies a position or phase before inspecting signal values. Phase locates progress through a repeating cycle; an unwrapped phase may increase past 1. An integer phase k means a whole number of cycles. A comb is a list of these event locations, often drawn as narrow marks. Its purpose is to compare responses at geometrically declared positions without allowing the response to move the marks.

Our synthetic coordinate is distance z(t)=t metres when t is in seconds, with origin z=0 at t=0, normal +z and spacing 2 metres. More explicitly the speed is 1 metre/second. Hence q=z/(2 metres)=t/(2 seconds) cycles. The shorthand q=t/2 assumes the numerical t is in seconds. This is a toy trajectory, not a physical celestial model. Solve q=k: t=2k seconds. In the closed midpoint range [0,4] seconds, k=0,1,2 gives events 0,2,4 seconds.

Freeze a mapping rule: use exact existing integer-second midpoint samples at offsets -1,0,+1 second, do not interpolate, and retain absent support. The offsets describe a three-sample window, not the continuous exposure durations. Exposure intervals remain separate metadata. At center 2, samples are at 1,2,3 seconds, giving raw A=(5,8,1). Side difference 5-1=4 tests same polarity; side sum 5+1=6 tests inverted polarity. The center 8 is also retained: under reversal it contributes same component 8, inverted component 0.

The full windows are center 0: (absent,2,5); center 2: (5,8,1); center 4: (1,3,absent). Their raw support masks are (0,1,1),(1,1,1),(1,1,0), where 1 means an acquired value exists. Row 3's stipulated diagnostic flag travels with both windows that use it. A separate normalized-validity mask is (0,1,0),(0,1,1),(1,1,0). None of the three ratio windows is complete. Do not fill the absent edge with zero or divide by zero to make the windows look alike.

A sidereal event is tied to orientation relative to celestial directions. A real calculation needs an observer site, time scale, astronomical position source (ephemeris), its version, vector origin, orientation frame, crossing equation and calibration interval. A direction alone is not a timestamp. Geometry and fitted response parameters have different roles: a calibration run may estimate response, while a later check run uses frozen parameters. Changing a center after seeing values creates a new exploratory analysis version.''',
'''A mean is the sum divided by the number of included entries. It represents a constant component over that declared support. Let m be the mean and d_i=x_i-m the detail at position i. The reconstruction x_i=m+d_i is an exact identity. A negative detail means below the chosen mean, not a negative raw count. Detail is retained information, not waste.

For the full five-row A support, sum=2+5+8+1+3=19 and m=19/5. Subtract 19/5 from each value: d=(-9/5,6/5,21/5,-14/5,-4/5). Their sum is zero because -9+6+21-14-4=0. Reconstruct row 2 as 19/5+21/5=40/5=8. The raw row with failed normalization remains in this raw mean; the view is explicitly all five supplied raw values including the stipulated fault flag.

For support rows 1,2,3 only, the sum is 14 and mean 14/3. Details are (1/3,10/3,-11/3). A changed mean can come from changed support rather than a new physical process. Always report the keys, time range, masks and weighting rule beside a mean.

A scale view groups information over a chosen span. For example, pair values (2,5): their mean is 3.5 and details (-1.5,1.5). The coarser mean plus the two details restores the pair. If you average a window or smooth a sequence, preserve the removed field and the exact boundary rule. At edges our rule is no invented padding. If a partial-support mean is requested, declare its smaller support explicitly.

Frequency coefficients from lesson 4 are another representation. Phase is the position within a cycle, represented by the signs and real/imaginary coordinates of those coefficients. Magnitude measures size while losing that position. For example a=(1,0,0,0) gives F=(1,1,1,1), while b=(0,1,0,0) gives F=(1,-i,-1,i). Both have four magnitudes of 1, yet their nonzero samples occur at different positions. Keeping complex coefficients with the inverse factor recovers the difference.

Across channels, retain each channel's response identity, raw scale, normalized scale, common support and separate masks. Similar-looking curves may invite a proposed relationship, but the transformation record must still distinguish them. Descriptive scores can index interesting windows; they cannot replace the signed fields that produced them.''',
'''An uncertainty record names what remains unresolved about the measurement and its interpretation. Different contributors have different structures: a shared clock offset moves all channel times together; an exposure covers an interval; a calibration factor may have a stated range; a gap is missing acquisition support; an unresolved wavelength dependence is a channel pattern. One number cannot describe all of these operations.

For a finite bound exercise, suppose factor k is in [1.9,2.1] units/count and input x in [0.9,1.1] counts. “Units” is a deliberately unspecified output unit, not a claim of watts or joules. Assume every combination inside this rectangular box is permitted. Both factors are positive, so the smallest product uses both lower endpoints: 1.9*0.9=1.71 units. The largest uses both upper endpoints: 2.1*1.1=2.31 units. The nominal product 2*1=2 lies inside. If factors can be negative, compute all four endpoint products before selecting bounds. If their dependence forbids some combinations, this box may be conservative; do not silently claim independent repeated observations from it.

Now suppose all midpoint times have one common unknown clock offset delta_t in [-0.1,0.1] seconds. Nominal event midpoint 2 maps to [1.9,2.1]. Exposure duration 0.2 has half-width 0.1, so possible exposure support runs from 1.9-0.1=1.8 to 2.1+0.1=2.2 seconds. This is a union of possible supports, not a claim that each exposure lasts 0.4 seconds. The common offset cancels in time differences between simultaneous channels.

Record orientation and angular resolution as unresolved if they are not supplied; do not invent bounds. Calibration missing, zero Clear, suspected saturation and absent sequence 5 are separate ledger fields. The zero count is still known even when its ratio cannot be formed.

Source card T35 keeps radiometric calibration (a link to optical quantities), exposure transfer (how time integration affects a signal), raw bytes, blank/reference measurements, angular uncertainty and an independent replay as open obligations. Its reported photon-equivalent model input is not an apparatus measurement of transferred energy. T50 additionally describes equal outputs from distinct gain/responsivity factorizations: if output is a product, the same product alone need not identify each factor. This is an identifiability problem. For a small illustration, 2*6=3*4=12; output 12 cannot select which pair produced it. These boundaries identify the measurements a prospective experiment needs.''',
'''A prospective protocol is a dated, versioned instruction set fixed before new outcomes are examined. It specifies what will be measured, how rows will be identified, what event geometry will be used, which transformations will be applied, and what ends the run. A synthetic rehearsal tests whether those instructions are executable. It does not carry out the future physical experiment.

Build the protocol in order. First state a target: record channel-resolved responses around independently specified crossings, retaining both polarity comparisons and all residual fields. Then freeze instrument response identities, orientation, gain, exposure, calibration references and time conventions. Next declare raw keys, fault statuses and missing-sample rules. Freeze the geometry version and event mapping before accessing check-run values. If an earlier interval trained amplitudes or widths, name that interval and freeze the resulting parameters; do not tune them on the later check interval.

Protocol Toy-v1 rehearses the five-row acquisition. It uses q=t/(2 seconds), exact offsets (-1,0,1) seconds and no interpolation. It retains all three event windows, both defects where defined, raw and ratio masks, mean/detail pairs and the independent four-point parity exercise. It records zero Clear and the stipulated saturation flag separately. The parity exercise is a separate periodic synthetic input, not four samples silently taken from the five-row acquisition. Every output is labeled synthetic.

For a future 10-second rehearsal, specify midpoint interval [0,10) seconds with 1-second cadence: exactly ten expected midpoints 0 through 9. Exposure is 0.2 seconds, so the first exposure begins at -0.1 seconds relative to the first midpoint. Start acquisition early enough to cover it, or retain the missing support if it cannot be covered. Stop after the tenth scheduled midpoint, or record an interruption and every acquired row. Endpoints are part of a protocol, not a formatting detail.

A real forward version remains planned until its physical site, UTC epoch, ephemeris, crossing contract and calibration are supplied. Keep a blank/reference control with the same declared settings and label any difference in apparatus configuration. Retain opposite-sign responses, no apparent match, instrument failures and interruptions along with matches. Report observed records, derived arithmetic, inferred relationships, proposed mechanisms and unresolved contributors in separate fields. Reference theory can guide a comparison without replacing these records. If revision is needed after inspection, preserve v1 and call v2 exploratory until tested on a new later run.'''
]

questions=[
 [('What interval supports row 4?','4 ± 0.1 gives [3.9,4.1] s.'),('Do equal counts with gain 1 and gain 2 establish equal incident energy?','No. The gain/calibration relations are needed; preserve both settings.'),('Why retain a response identity beside 500 nm?','A nominal wavelength does not specify the full accepted wavelength response.'),('What is known when calibration is absent?','The counts, settings, orientation and timing remain known; an optical-power conversion is unresolved.')],
 [('How does a recorded zero differ from a gap?','Zero is an acquired numeric value; a gap records an expected but absent acquisition.'),('Can two channels at one timestamp share one row key?','They need distinct channel identities within their keys; keep both.'),('What stays beside suspected saturation at row 3?','Raw A=1, Clear=10, sequence/time and the stipulated diagnostic reason remain.'),('How do you restore a display ordered 2,0,1?','Use retained sequence labels to order 0,1,2; never infer sequence only from timestamp.')],
 [('Compute A=0,C=5 and A=0,C=0.','First ratio is valid 0; second is undefined and retains both raw zeros.'),('Can r=1/5 alone recover A?','No: A=2,C=10 and A=4,C=20 both work. Retain C.'),('What is the missing numerator when r=2/5,C=20?','A=(2/5)*20=8 counts.'),('How is 1/3 reconstructed from its ten-digit display?','Add delta=1/30000000000 to 0.3333333333, then multiply by C=3 to return 1.')],
 [('Find both defects for (1,2,4,3).','Same=(-2,-2,2,2); inverted=(4,6,6,4). Neither vanishes.'),('Does H equal R for a four-point list?','No: H sends (1,2,3,4) to (3,4,1,2); R gives (4,3,2,1).'),('Can an even comparator detect o=(2,0,-2,0)?','No. (p,q,p,q) dot o=2p-2p=0 for every p,q; retain o.'),('What does the four-point DFT establish about native 25 and 27?','It aliases them modulo 4, so cannot resolve them as native harmonics. The proposed 25–27→2 mechanism remains a distinct source claim.'),('What must survive if an odd-channel comparison score is zero?','The odd component, its two complex coefficients, support and inverse rule; zero may reflect comparator blindness.')],
 [('Solve q=k over midpoint range [0,4].','t=2k seconds gives 0,2,4 for k=0,1,2.'),('What is the raw center-2 side difference and sum?','5-1=4 and 5+1=6; retain the center 8 and flags.'),('May the missing sample at -1 second be set to zero?','No. Preserve a missing-support label and the partial window.'),('What physical inputs does q=t/2 lack?','A physical site, UTC epoch/time scale, ephemeris/version, orientation and event definition; the toy rule supplies none of them.')],
 [('Reconstruct row 3 from full mean/detail.','19/5-14/5=1 count.'),('What is the sum of full details?','(-9+6+21-14-4)/5=0.'),('Why is 14/3 different from 19/5?','It is computed over rows 1,2,3 instead of all five. Its input support differs.'),('Do equal Fourier magnitudes imply equal sample positions?','No. The two single-pulse examples have equal magnitudes and different complex coefficients; retain phase.')],
 [('Multiply the positive lower endpoints.','1.9*0.9=1.71 output units; upper endpoints give 2.31.'),('Does possible support [1.8,2.2] imply exposure duration 0.4?','No. Each exposure lasts 0.2; midpoint uncertainty shifts its location.'),('Do two simultaneous channels have independent clock offsets here?','No. Both share delta_t, which cancels in their time difference.'),('What lets counts become measured transferred energy?','An appropriate calibrated apparatus and exposure-transfer account with required inputs; a photon-equivalent model value alone does not supply this.')],
 [('How many midpoint samples belong to [0,10) at 1-second cadence?','Ten: 0 through 9. Endpoint 10 is excluded.'),('What is the status of the five-row rehearsal?','Executed synthetic arithmetic; no performed physical measurement is claimed.'),('What happens if v1 mismatches a channel?','Keep the mismatch and its residuals; retain v1. Any tuned v2 is separately labeled exploratory.'),('Which outcomes enter the final ledger?','Every match, opposite sign, weak response, mismatch, invalid transform, gap, fault and interruption, with input identities.')]
]

